course: dictionary
lesson: 12053
title: Sound words in stories
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Some words sound like the thing they describe. Clack is one of them: say it out loud and you can almost hear two hard things knocking together. Writers use words like this to help you hear a scene, not just picture it.
explain: «Her heels made a clack on the hard floor as she walked to the stage.» The word clack tells you the sound is short, sharp and hard. Hear it in your head, then ask: would a soft word like «whisper» fit here? It would not, so clack is the right choice.
explain: Clack can be a verb or a noun. «The knitting needles clacked all evening» means they made the sound again and again. «The clack of the old typewriter filled the room» uses clack as a noun, meaning the sound itself. Look at the sentence to see which one you have.
explain: Clack has an informal meaning too. «My cousin would clack on the phone for hours» means she chatted loudly and on and on. When a word has more than one meaning, the sentence tells you which one is right.
fact: Clack is a sound word: it sounds like the noise it makes.
fact: «Clack» means to make a short, sharp sound, like two hard things hitting each other.
fact: As a noun, «a clack» or «the clack» means the sound itself.
fact: Informal: «clack» can also mean to chatter loudly and on and on.
fact: Similar words: «click» and «clatter». A click is usually one small sound, while a clatter is a noisy rattle of many sounds.
exercise: «The heels made a clack on the hard floor.» What does clack mean here? | Something very cold | A long song | A soft, gentle sigh | *A short, sharp sound
exercise: «The clack of the typewriter filled the room.» In this sentence, what kind of word is clack? | *A noun, meaning the sound itself | A question word | A verb, meaning to sleep | An adjective, meaning bright
exercise: Which word is most similar in meaning to clack? | Sunny | Whisper | Melt | *Click
test: «My cousin would clack on the phone all evening about her new bike.» What does clack mean here? | To fall asleep quietly | To drop something heavy | *To chatter loudly and on and on | To write a long letter
test: «The knitting needles clacked as Grandma worked.» What does clacked show? | The needles were soft and silent | The needles were broken | *The needles made short, sharp sounds again and again | Grandma stopped knitting
exam: Which sentence uses clack as a verb? | *The gate clacked shut behind the dog. | The clack of his shoes echoed down the hall. | Clack is my favourite word. | A clack is a sound that many people know.
